title: Single-root content view from ID
pid: a85ddf73-e425-4928-8089-89272426f931
body: |2-
    - Request: =((request . "single root content view")
      (id . "NODE_ID") (view-id . "VIEW_ID")
      (approved-overPrivateText-pids "PID" ...))=
    - Response: LP =((response-type content-view) (content "...")
      (errors ("..." ...)) (warnings ("..." ...)))=. The document
      structure is detailed below, under =Content view=.
      Under a restricted skgrepo-set, a response involving an overPrivateText
      telescope instead returns LP =overPrivateText-telescope-confirmation= with
      =(operation single-root-view)=, the exact PIDs, and a text-free
      prompt. An approved retry carries those
      exact PIDs in =approved-overPrivateText-pids=; approval is not cached.
      An unrestricted connection receives the ordinary response with a
      warning.
    - If =NODE_ID= resolves to a restricted skgrepo, the server refuses the
      request with a human-readable message and does not open a buffer.
      Following a link to a restricted-skgrepo node behaves the same way.
    - If =NODE_ID= names no node of the graph, the response is an
      ordinary content-view whose =content= is empty, with no errors and
      no warnings. An unknown viewnode stands for an ID its viewparent's
      list names (see "ghost, unknown viewnode" in =docs/glossary.org=);
      no list names a root, so the ID rule makes it a tombstone, which
      the refresh prunes. Under a skgrepo restriction the request is
      refused instead, as for a restricted node.
    - If =NODE_ID= is a root of an open content view proper (a content
      view that is no buffer of the diff view; see "view, buffer,
      viewforest" in =docs/glossary.org=), the
      response is LP =((response-type content-view) (switch-to-view
      VIEW_ID))=, naming that view, and the client switches to its
      buffer; nothing is rendered. A node that is a root only of buffers
      of the diff view gets a new content view.
    - The requested node opens as itself: the view's perspective is the
      root homes, where the root's own home skgrepo decides, and there
      the node stands as itself. Its content substitutes as any
      content does. See "Mirror substitution" below. Fork facts can be
      inspected in the heralds (the K token).
